#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93911837-1abd-5695-838e-0d78fbca8630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098515} {:answer/id #uuid "af696adf-49eb-5094-96ca-2aec3c107333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098516} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4674ff-714d-5ade-b3a0-42e6ee63bd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098517} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74b8c9-fb4a-547e-b007-c516090fccf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098518} {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb3e85e-b511-5963-8b3f-72d966e1c836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81bb9a03-c337-5557-bf63-9b6b72dc52f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098521} {:answer/id #uuid "8dafe98c-e0e8-5d1a-8ace-80fcf41a6d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098522} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaaa4e9-cb9d-55d2-88a9-f8a2c63b0d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098523} {:answer/id #uuid "e7061204-ab95-516a-9a50-4a5fdc5a1efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098524} {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525} :answer-field/id #uuid "25da4b65-0173-565c-ba8e-416b6d42051b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9e7a06-a7ad-5fea-bd64-541162aae1b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01251fc0-f57a-5dfd-8833-fe87964205c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"}]}
